  "meta": {
    "title": "The Soldier Who Could Never Be Full (True Story)",
    "description": "In 1790s France, a soldier named Tarrare could eat almost anything, and the army tried to turn that into a weapon. A true, little-known story from military history.",
    "hashtags": [
      "#darkhistory",
      "#truestory",
      "#history",
      "#creepyhistory",
      "#animatedhistory",
      "#shorts"
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "claim": "Tarrare's extreme appetite: he reportedly ate live animals including a live cat, and a meal prepared for 15 labourers at the hospital",
        "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tarrare' (draws on Percy's 1804 Memoire sur la polyphagie and 19th-century compilations: Lord 1839, Good 1864); Mental Floss, 'Tarrare, the Greatest Glutton of All Time' (via search)",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "Percy's 1804 memoir is the primary source; everything repeated today derives from it. Narration says 'reportedly' for the feast. The abnormally wide mouth is also documented."
      },
      {
        "claim": "He swallowed a wooden box holding a document, as a courier to a French colonel held by the Prussians, and was captured, whipped and threatened with hanging (noose at a gallows) before being released",
        "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tarrare'; Amusing Planet, 'Tarrare: The Man Who Ate Too Much' and The Line-Up (via search)",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "ATTRIBUTION: Wikipedia (following Percy) says the courier idea was proposed by Dr Courville, working with Percy and General de Beauharnais; some retellings credit Percy. The recorded narration says 'a surgeon named Pierre Percy had a dark idea', which sources do not support cleanly. The 'secret message' was a dummy note, and whether the box was recovered is disputed."
      },
      {
        "claim": "He asked Percy for a cure and treatments failed; he died of tuberculosis at Versailles in 1798, aged about 26; autopsy: abnormally wide gullet, enormous stomach filling most of the abdomen",
        "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tarrare'; Amusing Planet and Bored Panda (via search)",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "AGE: his birth date is unknown and 26 is a doctors' estimate (infobox says 25-26). The recorded narration says 'just twenty-six' as fact; should be 'about twenty-six'. 'Begged' is slightly stronger than the sources ('he told Percy he would try any cure')."
      },
      {
        "claim": "The golden fork: Tarrare himself claimed he had swallowed a golden fork; none was found at the autopsy",
        "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tarrare' ('presented as his own belief'; 'No fork was found at autopsy')",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "Narration ('Some say he even swallowed a golden fork') is acceptably hedged. Hook line 'the army turned his stomach into a secret weapon' is loose: the army used him as a courier, not a weapon."
      }
    ]
  }
}